From: gts7l project
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: double the per-CRTC frame-event pool (4 -> 8)

Under sustained heavy 3D load (Minecraft), this device logged
"[drm:dpu_crtc_frame_event_cb] *ERROR* crtcN event 1 overflow" storms
with hundreds of suppressed callbacks, right before the (since fixed)
memory-pressure hard lockups. Most likely those storms were a symptom:
the SCHED_FIFO event thread stalled behind a wedged CPU. The real hang
was the DTS memory-node hole (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md, "CPU
hard-hang, root cause found").

Kept as cheap headroom, since every post-fix Minecraft soak (2026-09-22)
ran with it and showed no overflows. Previously lived only in the
work/linux tree; captured here so a fresh tree reproduces the tested
kernel.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_crtc.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_crtc.h
@@ -16,8 +16,32 @@
 
 #define DPU_CRTC_NAME_SIZE	12
 
-/* define the maximum number of in-flight frame events */
-#define DPU_CRTC_FRAME_EVENT_SIZE	4
+/*
+ * gts7l (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md, real crash investigation
+ * 2026-09-20/21): confirmed on real hardware, twice, under sustained
+ * heavy 3D rendering load (Minecraft) - this fixed 4-slot pool runs
+ * dry ([drm:dpu_crtc_frame_event_cb] *ERROR* crtcN event 1 overflow,
+ * hundreds of suppressed callbacks), followed by a genuine
+ * watchdog-detected hard CPU lockup. The event-thread kthread worker
+ * that drains this pool is correctly configured as a real SCHED_FIFO
+ * thread (dpu_kms.c's sched_set_fifo() call) and CPU frequency scaling
+ * was independently confirmed working (ruling out plain CPU
+ * starvation as the sole cause) - the crash recurred identically even
+ * after fixing a real, separate cpufreq bug
+ * (CONFIG_INTERCONNECT_QCOM_OSM_L3), so this pool's small fixed size
+ * is a real, confirmed bottleneck under real-world load this specific
+ * mainline driver version was apparently never stress-tested against.
+ * Doubled rather than picked arbitrarily larger - a low-risk bit of
+ * headroom to absorb bursts.
+ *
+ * Update 2026-09-22: the hard lockups turned out to be a separate,
+ * now-fixed bug (the board DTS declared a firmware memory hole as RAM,
+ * see docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md "CPU hard-hang, root cause found"),
+ * so the overflow storms were most likely a symptom of a CPU wedging
+ * behind this pool's drain thread, not the cause. Kept anyway: it costs a
+ * few bytes, and every post-fix Minecraft soak ran with it.
+ */
+#define DPU_CRTC_FRAME_EVENT_SIZE	8
 
 /**
  * enum dpu_crtc_client_type: crtc client type
